decisions:
  - id: enforce-deny-by-default
    decision: enforce() now deny-by-default for notebook boundaries (notebook-promotion, notebook-publish)
    rationale: Security audit found both fell through to allowed:true. Write-path boundaries must never default to allow.
  - id: bidirectional-relay-filtering
    decision: Ring 1 content filtering on both incoming AND outbox watcher in Symphony relay
    rationale: 'Security audit: filtering only on receipt is insufficient — outbound messages from local agents could still leak project-locked content to peers.'
  - id: health-dot-rootdir
    decision: Pass rootDir to getHealthDot() so it reads actual violation history
    rationale: 'Reviewer caught: without rootDir, recentRate stays 0, making stable+violations show green instead of yellow.'
  - id: aliases-not-matching-engine
    decision: Tool aliases are metadata on tool definitions, not a matching engine rewrite
    rationale: Aliases are visible to LLMs in tool descriptions. Simple string-includes in tags.ts for tag-based search. No need for embedding pipeline at 50 tools.
  - id: compliance-checks-proxy
    decision: Stop hook compliance snapshot uses SOURCE_COUNT+PARADIGM_COUNT as proxy for checks run
    rationale: Reviewer noted this is a file count proxy, not actual check count. Acceptable for v1 trend analysis — a 2x inflation in checks doesn't affect trend direction.
learnings:
  - Security agent running in parallel with builder catches architectural gaps the builder misses — enforce() allow-by-default and one-sided relay filtering were both invisible to the builder but critical blockers
  - Reviewer caught a functional bug (missing rootDir argument) that would have made health dots meaningless — the two-stage review protocol works
  - Builder agents can handle 3 features in one pass if they share the same codebase area, but they reliably miss 'negative space' requirements (things that should NOT exist, like scoring in P4)
  - 'Tool aliases as metadata-on-definitions is the right v1: LLMs see them in tool listings, tags.ts does string-includes matching, zero new infrastructure. Revisit only if tool count exceeds 200'
  - The shell stop hook is the right place for compliance snapshots — it runs on every session end, has access to VIOLATION_COUNT, and printf-to-JSONL is zero-dependency
